@title Ticketing platform architecture
@subtitle Azure, North Europe · Owner: Digital Channels · Reviewed 2026-09-22

## Overview
Passengers book through Azure Front Door. Every booking is written to one database and announced as an event, so that the scanners at the terminals stay in step.

## Diagram
diagram tix dir=LR title="Ticketing on Azure"
tix: pass "Passengers" user
tix: fd "Front Door" lb
tix: web "Booking Web App" svc
tix: api "Ticketing API" svc focus
tix: db "Reservations DB" db "Azure SQL"
tix: ev "Booking Events" queue "Service Bus"
tix: scan "Port Terminal Scanners" ext
tix: pay "Payment Provider" ext
tix: group appsn "App subnet 10.50.1.0/24" web, api
tix: group datasn "Data subnet 10.50.2.0/24" db, ev
tix: group vnet "Ticketing VNet 10.50.0.0/16" appsn, datasn
tix: group sub "Ticketing subscription" vnet
tix: pass -> fd : HTTPS
tix: fd -> web : origin request
tix: web -> api : REST
tix: api -> db : SQL, port 1433
tix: api -> pay : card authorization
tix: api -> ev : ticket issued
tix: ev -> scan : sync every 30 s

## Capacity
- **Booking Web App**: 3 to 10 instances at 60 percent CPU
- **Ticketing API**: 4 to 16 pods
- **Reservations DB**: 8 vCores, with a read replica in the paired region

## Recovery targets
kpi Recovery point objective | 5 minutes | maximum data loss | neutral
kpi Recovery time objective | 1 hour | maximum downtime | neutral
kpi Last tested | 2026-08-19 | | neutral
